values
  (221, 'How slice 4.4 is built (`slice-4-4.md`), from G16. A goal is a job of kind `goal` that a signed-in person makes in the web app for a thread the bot is already in (one goal per thread), with its tickets listed in order (1 to 30), a limit on open changes (1 to 5, two unless said) and a cap. Each wake settles the changes it started (a stopped change, a merged or closed pull request read from GitHub with the checks token; a merged ticket''s built-in task is marked done as the goal, a Jira ticket is left for a person to close), and, under the limit, writes the next ticket''s brief with one call to Claude Opus 5.5 (no tools, as 219) and posts it in the thread. Every goal brief needs a person to press Start. At most one brief per wake; a ticket is briefed once', 'People list the tickets and own the goal (G16), so a goal cannot widen its own scope. The goal reads tickets anyone with access to the tracker can write, so, as 2.1''s rule 3, the only path from that text to the worker is a brief a person has seen and started. The API only reads Jira (3.3), so it does not close Jira tickets', 'active', 'the owner', '2026-11-26T00:00:00Z', 'import', '221'),
  (222, 'Changes to slice 4.4 after an independent review, before it merged. A stopped change whose pull request GitHub did not answer for is left for a later wake, so a stop never hides a merge. A pull request closed while its change still has work in hand (a brief not yet handed to the worker, or a worker on one) holds its ticket''s place, since another pull request may follow; it settles as closed once the change is idle or stopped. A wake that fails after its model call keeps the call''s cost. Changing a goal locks its tickets and removes only tickets never briefed, so a ticket briefed while a person changes the goal stays in it. Left as they are: a pull request in another repository is never read, so its ticket holds its place until its change is stopped; a brief kept under its key whose change a stop ended meanwhile is still posted, and its ticket is then settled as stopped; overlapping wakes of a goal woken every minute can pay twice for one brief, though it is kept and posted once; the web app''s job page needs this slice''s API. Reconcile runs every due job''s wake in turn, and a goal''s wake can take over a minute, so the function''s time limit must allow the checker''s and the goals'' wakes together', 'The review found that a GitHub error at the wrong time could settle a merged ticket as stopped and leave its task open, that a pull request closed before another try freed its ticket''s place early, letting the goal open more changes than its limit and miss the later merge, that a failure after the model call lost its cost, and that a ticket briefed while a person removed it could be dropped from the goal. Each now has a test', 'active', 'the owner', '2026-11-27T00:00:00Z', 'import', '222')
on conflict (origin, origin_ref) where origin_ref is not null do nothing;
